          { name: "Education Funding & School Quality", tag: "lean-into", why: "In a district that is 50.3% college-educated with $147,535 median household income and 88.1% homeowner concentration, education system performance is the single most direct measure of whether government is delivering tangible value to the constituency. Parent satisfaction with school funding, teacher compensation, and academic outcomes is not an ideological issue — it is a material quality-of-life issue that directly affects property values and family economic planning. An incumbent who loses the education governance narrative loses the primary legitimacy test with his base." },
          { name: "Property Tax Fairness & Homeowner Relief", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $147,535 income with 88.1% homeownership, AD-8 is a constituency of affluent homeowners acutely aware of property tax burden and highly engaged in tax policy debates. Whether local property tax rates are rising faster than household income, whether assessment methodologies are perceived as fair, and whether the incumbent has a coherent property tax relief framework are concrete material concerns that drive base engagement or defection. An incumbent who cannot articulate specific property tax policy positions is vulnerable to opposition messaging that frames him as indifferent to homeowner economic security." },
          { name: "Minority Professional Community Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "The 5.15% Asian and 11.44% Hispanic populations are individually small but together represent roughly 16.6% of the district — and appear concentrated among college-educated professionals and younger households. These communities are not naturally Democratic in affluent suburban contexts, but they are persuadable on education equity, hiring and advancement fairness in local institutions, and economic opportunity. In a 66-34 race, consistent neglect or marginal engagement with these communities creates an opportunity for opposition to consolidate minority professional voters around specific governance concerns." },
          { name: "Treating 66-34 as a Durable Coalition Rather Than a Conditional Consolidation", tag: "avoid", why: "A 32-point margin is mathematically large and reflects genuine demographic alignment with Republican voting, but it depends entirely on the incumbent maintaining consolidation of the college-educated homeowner majority without notable defection. This is not a safe seat because the base is ideologically locked — it is a consolidation seat because the base is materially satisfied with governance outcomes on education, taxes, and infrastructure. If the incumbent allows these governance narratives to be controlled by opposition, the base can shift." },
          { name: "Positioning as a Cultural Conservative on Identity Issues in a College-Educated, High-Income District", tag: "avoid", why: "AD-8's college-educated majority (50.3%) and professional demographics make it sensitive to candidates who lead on cultural grievances or position opposition to civil rights and education inclusion as primary campaign themes. This constituency evaluates primarily on education quality, tax fairness, and infrastructure — not on abstract culture-war positioning. Any incumbent who allows the campaign narrative to shift toward identity politics rather than education and tax governance is playing to opposition strengths and away from his own base's actual priorities." }
