          { name: "Economic Mobility and Equity in Public Institutions", tag: "lean-into", why: "The Black-Latino majority (89.5% combined) in a district with a 35.9-year median age and 23% college attainment is a working-age, family-centered constituency that evaluates on whether education, public safety, and economic development are delivering equity and opportunity for their children. School quality, police accountability, local economic development that creates stable employment, and the pathway from education to jobs are the concrete measures by which this constituency assesses whether representatives are serving them. Candidates who address these material concerns with specificity rather than abstract partisan messaging connect more authentically to what drives constituent preference and turnout." },
          { name: "Constituent Service and Accessibility", tag: "careful", why: "In a primary-competitive district where the general election margin is so large that the real contest is among Democrats, constituent service and accessibility become primary-election differentiators. An incumbent who is perceived as difficult to reach, slow to respond to community concerns, or taking constituent loyalty for granted becomes vulnerable to a primary challenger who can credibly claim to be more responsive. For a working-age, renter-heavy, family-centered constituency with real material concerns, accessibility and responsiveness are not secondary political assets but primary-election factors." },
          { name: "Treating the 82% Margin as General Election Security", tag: "avoid", why: "An 82% general election result is not a safe seat in the ordinary sense — it is a primary mandate and a measure of current constituent alignment, not an insulation from challenge. In a district where the Democratic margin is so dominant that meaningful electoral competition occurs in the primary, any incumbent who treats the 82% result as permission to reduce constituent engagement or attention to material concerns is vulnerable to a credible primary challenger who can convince the Black-Latino majority that they would serve them more responsively." },
          { name: "Allowing Housing, Economic Mobility, or Public Institution Concerns to Become Secondary", tag: "avoid", why: "A representative who addresses housing, economic opportunity, and public institution performance as secondary issues — treating them as side concerns rather than core constituent material — loses the trust of a Black-Latino majority that is directly evaluating whether public systems are delivering for their families. For renters worried about displacement, working-age parents concerned about school quality and job pathways, and community members evaluating police accountability, these are primary concerns, not peripheral ones. A legislative agenda that marginalizes them in favor of abstract partisan positioning is one vulnerable to primary challenge." }
