          { name: "Treating Demographic Homogeneity as an Electoral Advantage", tag: "avoid", why: "While 64.22% white, 14.63% Asian, 14.04% Hispanic, and 3.11% Black creates a majority-white district, the combined 32% minority population is both real and politically active. A campaign strategy that relies on implicit appeals to white identity or treats minority communities as peripheral constituencies is not only ethically problematic but strategically dangerous — it cedes opportunities to connect with educated, affluent Asian, Hispanic, and Black professionals who have genuine economic stakes in the district and may be persuadable on Republican economic policy if engaged authentically." },
          { name: "Assuming Wealth and Education Guarantee Loyalty", tag: "avoid", why: "Affluent, college-educated voters are capable of sophisticated policy analysis and are less likely to vote based on inherited party affiliation than on material evaluation of governance outcomes. A candidate who takes the district's Republican majority for granted and fails to demonstrate ongoing value delivery — or who relies on cultural grievance rather than concrete economic policy — risks losing persuadable voters within the professional class to a challenger who articulates a more compelling case for why their governance better serves this constituency's interests." }
