          { name: "Public Safety and Crime Accountability", tag: "lean-into", why: "Urban districts at this income level have historically been sensitive to public safety messaging, and the 62% Democratic margin in NY-AD-22 requires that Black and Latino communities — 48.17% of the population — view the incumbent as actively working to reduce crime and hold police accountable simultaneously. The incumbent must lead on specific crime reduction metrics, community policing investments, and criminal justice reform rather than allow the opposition to define the safety debate. A narrative that connects police accountability with effective public safety — not a false choice — is essential to holding both the professional base and communities of color." },
          { name: "Racial Diversity and Coalition Integrity", tag: "careful", why: "No single racial or ethnic community comprises a clear majority in NY-AD-22 (white 29.18%, Black 26.74%, Latino 21.43%, Asian 17.18%), making coalition management the structural requirement of electoral success. The risk is that an incumbent can over-invest in one community while taking others for granted, or that messaging becomes siloed by community, creating the perception that different groups are being told different things. Authentic engagement requires that the incumbent's core message — about fiscal responsibility, education, and opportunity — resonates across all four communities with specificity and consistency, rather than separating into discrete racial-demographic campaigns." },
          { name: "Tax Burden Messaging That Sacrifices Public Investment", tag: "avoid", why: "A common Republican opening in high-income urban districts is to attack Democratic tax policy as unsustainable while promising fiscal conservatism — and in a district where 79.2% are homeowners with significant property tax exposure, this messaging can gain traction. The incumbent must avoid ceding the fiscal responsibility frame to Republicans by accepting the premise that taxes are inherently the problem; instead, the frame should center on value delivered per tax dollar and the consequences of underinvestment in schools, infrastructure, and public safety. Accepting Republican framing on taxes as burden rather than investment in shared prosperity is the pathway to losing affluent professional voters who would otherwise remain Democratic." },
          { name: "Treating Minority Communities as Monolithic or Separate from the Core Message", tag: "avoid", why: "An incumbent can lose a 62% margin by allowing Black and Latino community engagement to become disconnected from the broader campaign message — appearing to 'reach out' to these communities in isolation rather than integrating their economic and civic concerns into a unified vision of the district's future. When messaging on school funding, public safety, economic mobility, and fiscal responsibility remains disconnected from community-specific outreach, it signals that the incumbent views minority communities as constituencies to manage rather than as core parts of the district whose concerns are central to governing. This vulnerability is particularly acute in a district where Black and Latino voters together comprise 48.17% of the population and are essential to the 62% baseline." }
