          { name: "Treating Unopposed Electoral Dominance as Evidence of Constituent Satisfaction", tag: "avoid", why: "A 100-0 result is a political liability masquerading as a mandate: it tells you nothing about whether the incumbent is actually serving the 50.54% Black and 21.84% Asian population, responding to their material concerns, or delivering on economic development and public safety. Incumbents who interpret unopposed elections as evidence of deep constituent support often become complacent, underinvest in constituent service, and create the exact conditions that allow a better-organized challenger to build support in a primary where engagement and responsiveness become the decisive factors." },
          { name: "Ignoring Renter and Working-Professional Concerns in a Homeowner-Heavy District", tag: "avoid", why: "While the 24.9% renter rate reflects a homeowner-majority district, it also indicates roughly one-quarter of the population navigating high urban housing costs without the property-stake investment that homeowners bring. Young professionals, immigrant families, and working-class renters have distinct concerns around housing affordability and tenant protections that can become invisible in district strategy focused only on homeowner constituencies. In a district where economic diversity exists alongside income stability, neglecting renter concerns creates a constituency gap that a primary challenger can exploit." }
