          { name: "Multiethnic Constituent Service & Community Parity", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 51% Latino plurality, 27% white, 16% Asian composition means no single community represents a demographic majority—requiring equally attentive constituent engagement across all three groups rather than strategic prioritization of one community over others. Immigration policy, language access, economic mobility pathways, and community safety concerns vary meaningfully across these communities, and a representative who frames one community's concerns as the district priority risks losing the multiethnic coalition that makes the seat safely Democratic. Constituent trust in a multiethnic urban district depends on demonstrable evidence that all communities receive equal attention and that community leaders across groups have direct access to their representative; treating any community as secondary generates organized opposition among underserved constituents." },
          { name: "Young Adult Economic Mobility & Opportunity", tag: "careful", why: "The 37.8-year median age combined with 33.7% college attainment signals a constituency of working adults and young professionals evaluating whether government is creating real pathways to economic stability—housing, childcare, education, and job access are the material concerns shaping political evaluation for this group. In a district with significant immigrant populations and working families, young adults' perception of whether they can build economic security in the neighborhood directly affects engagement and turnout; inattention to these concerns among younger constituents creates vulnerability to either lower engagement or opposition candidacies that specifically address young adult economic anxiety. The unopposed 2024 victory was partly enabled by lack of organized alternative; a future challenger who demonstrates superior attentiveness to young adult economic mobility could credibly mobilize this constituency." },
          { name: "Framing Housing as a Secondary Social Issue", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where 69% rent and housing cost burden is the primary economic pressure on constituent material conditions, treating housing policy as a secondary social justice concern rather than a central economic security issue is strategically tone-deaf to the material reality facing the majority of voters. Constituent evaluation is fundamentally economic in a renter-majority district: families are evaluating whether their representative is delivering policies that allow them to afford to stay in their homes and neighborhoods. A representative who defaults to abstract ideological framing around housing rather than concrete policy results on rent, tenant protections, and displacement prevention will appear disconnected from the economic vulnerability that shapes daily constituent experience." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed Victory as Durable Incumbent Security", tag: "avoid", why: "A 100%-0% 2024 result in a solidly Democratic district should not be mistaken for electoral invulnerability: unopposed races often reflect lack of organized alternative rather than genuine incumbent popularity, and in a renter-majority, multiethnic district with ongoing economic pressures, a credible challenger demonstrating superior attentiveness to housing affordability and constituent service could activate genuine opposition. The next cycle's competitive dynamics will depend heavily on whether Gonzalez-Rojas has built durable multiethnic constituent relationships and delivered visible results on housing affordability—the two issues most likely to generate organized opposition if they are perceived as underserved. Assuming a 100% result translates to safe 55-60% reelection margins would be a serious miscalculation of electoral risk in this constituency." }
