          { name: "Transit & Multimodal Urban Infrastructure", tag: "lean-into", why: "A college-educated, urban, 48.8% renter constituency depends on public transit for mobility and economic access; transit reliability, frequency, safety, and affordability directly affect quality of life and household cost structure for the majority of residents. MTA funding advocacy, transit-oriented development, and bus/subway service expansion in high-density renter neighborhoods are concrete governance metrics that this constituency evaluates. Visible incumbent work on transit improvement and investment — or visible failure to secure resources or fix service failures — generates measurable voter response." },
          { name: "Multiethnic Coalition Governance Without Dominant Majority", tag: "careful", why: "With 48.99% white, 22.71% Black, 16.16% Asian, 8.19% Hispanic, and approximately 4.27% in other categories, NY-AD-41 has no racial demographic majority and requires genuine inclusive governance across multiple communities rather than defaulting to any single group. Each community has distinct economic concerns — renter affordability and transit access transcend all groups, but anti-Black racism, immigrant economic mobility, and specific community health and safety issues require constituent-specific attention. An incumbent who consolidates any single demographic group while neglecting others creates room for a challenger who organizes the overlooked communities." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed Status as Guaranteed Reelection", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed 2024 result is a measure of current party consolidation and lack of organized opposition, not proof of unshakeable electoral strength or constituent satisfaction. In a district where housing affordability is the dominant material concern, a primary challenger who builds renter organizations and housing justice constituencies could emerge rapidly and pose serious threat. An incumbent who responds to unopposed status with complacency — reduced constituent service, lower legislative output on housing, or perceived alignment with luxury development over renter protection — creates the political opening that activates a primary." },
          { name: "Performative or Disconnected Urban Progressivism", tag: "avoid", why: "A college-educated, professional constituency evaluates political claims against measurable outcomes: housing units built and rented at affordable rates, transit service improvement metrics, displacement prevented, and small business support translated into neighborhood economic vitality are the concrete accountability measures. An incumbent who advances symbolic progressive positions while failing to deliver on housing affordability, allowing renter displacement to accelerate, or presiding over transit service decline risks the judgment from educated voters that they are not actually being represented — a perception that invites primary challenge and erodes volunteer enthusiasm and turnout." }
