          "Engage the 39.5% Asian population with specificity by community and language — Chinese, Korean, South Asian, and Southeast Asian residents have distinct business profiles, immigration sponsorship concerns, and healthcare access needs; treating 'Asian' as a single voting bloc means missing governance opportunities and vulnerabilities within the largest demographic group in the district.",
          "Address the 39% opposition directly on economic outcomes — in a Democratic-leaning district, the opposition is not primarily partisan but reflects voters who believe current representation is not delivering on affordability, wage growth, or service access; a representative who consolidates only the existing 61% and ignores what generated the 39% will find that margin eroding as cost-of-living pressures intensify.",
          "Build immigrant-serving infrastructure into constituent services — with a high proportion of first-generation immigrant households and non-English-speaking residents, a representative who ensures multilingual access to benefits, housing counseling, and small-business support is building the durable relationship that sustains a coalition in a district where economic mobility is the core organizing issue."
