          { name: "Working-Class Economic Security and Wage Policy", tag: "lean-into", why: "The $75,923 median income and 34.5% college attainment profile a working-class constituency for whom wage floors, benefit access, and cost-of-living relief are not abstract policy but direct household concerns. Local minimum wage policy, healthcare affordability, childcare subsidy access, and public transportation investment are the governance items that translate into tangible quality-of-life impact. Mitaynes's 77% reflects constituent satisfaction with responsive focus on these material concerns—and maintaining that satisfaction requires ongoing attention to wage and benefit policy rather than pivoting toward more affluent or educated constituencies." },
          { name: "Coalition Governance Across Diverse Minority Communities", tag: "careful", why: "The 44.73% Latino plurality is the dominant demographic force, but the 20.48% Asian and 7.55% Black communities represent meaningful constituencies whose distinct concerns around economic mobility, healthcare access, and community safety require specific attention rather than absorption into a broader 'communities of color' framework. In a 77-23 district, perceived inequality of service or attention between the Latino plurality and other minority communities can activate primary challenges or reduce engagement. The governance challenge is sustaining Latino coalition leadership while ensuring that Asian and Black constituents experience active responsiveness to their specific needs." },
          { name: "Treating the 77% Margin as a Permanent Safe Harbor", tag: "avoid", why: "A 77-23 result in a heavily Democratic district with no meaningful Republican alternative creates the false perception that the seat is completely secure—when the real competitive dynamic is internal to the Democratic coalition. A primary challenger with stronger connection to the Asian or Black communities, or with more aggressive housing and wage policy messaging, could shift coalition dynamics in a future cycle where turnout is lower or the incumbent is perceived as complacent. The 77% is sustainable only through active constituent engagement, not through the absence of electoral risk." },
          { name: "Adopting Abstract Progressive Positioning Without Constituent Service Delivery", tag: "avoid", why: "Working-class renters in a high-cost-of-living district evaluate representation on whether government is delivering concrete relief on housing, wages, and healthcare—not on ideological purity or symbolic progressive positioning. An incumbent who emphasizes abstract policy goals while neglecting constituent service, responsiveness to neighborhood-specific problems, or tangible delivery on rent stabilization and wage policy risks being perceived as out of touch with the material concerns that justify the 77% margin. This constituency rewards responsiveness and delivery; it punishes ideology without results." }
