          { name: "Job Quality & Wage Progression for Non-College-Credential Workforce", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 22.5% college attainment rate and sub-median $46,895 income describe a constituency whose economic mobility depends on job quality and wage growth in sectors that do not require four-year degrees — retail, service, healthcare support, transportation, construction, and skilled trades. Legislative focus on wage floors, benefits access, scheduling stability, and advancement pathways in these sectors is more strategically relevant than abstract small-business incentives or college-promotion programs that do not match the actual employment landscape. A representative who leads on concrete improvements to working conditions and compensation in jobs that actually employ this district's constituents demonstrates understanding of material economic constraints." },
          { name: "Erosion Risk in the 8% Republican Share & Primary Challenge Vulnerability", tag: "careful", why: "The 92% Democratic margin in a district with sustained economic pressure on a renter-majority, working-age population is not a guarantee of durability — it reflects current consolidation around Democratic representation, not immunity from challenge. Primary opponents who run on more aggressive economic agendas (deeper housing cost relief, bolder wage policies, healthcare expansion) can mobilize the constituency's legitimate frustration with material conditions. Any incumbent who assumes 92% is demographically inevitable and governs without continuously demonstrating material improvement in constituent economic security risks primary erosion; any challenger who wants to flip the seat must run on a platform of more specific and more aggressive economic delivery than the current representative." },
          { name: "Treating Housing as a Secondary Policy Concern", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where 83.4% are renters at $46,895 median income, housing cost burden is not a special interest or an ancillary policy domain — it is the primary economic constraint on constituent wellbeing. A representative who frames housing policy as something to be negotiated with developers and real estate interests rather than as a primary obligation to constituents is governing against the district's material reality. This positioning will be actively exploited by any challenger who leads on tenant protection and affordable housing development as the core economic issue." },
          { name: "Relying on the 92% Margin as Evidence of Unchecked Incumbent Power", tag: "avoid", why: "A 92% Democratic result in a district facing real economic pressure — renters, working-age population, sub-median income — is fragile if the incumbent does not continuously deliver material improvements in constituent economic security. Treating this margin as a license to deprioritize constituent service, to focus on higher office, or to govern without active accountability to the renter majority who cannot afford to move is the surest path to primary erosion or general-election vulnerability. The 92% baseline must be continuously earned through demonstrated commitment to reducing housing cost burden and improving job quality for a working population." }
