          { name: "Equitable Development & Community Benefit from Growth", tag: "lean-into", why: "With a 55% Black majority and 26% white, 8.67% Hispanic, and 5.71% Asian population, NY-AD-59 is a coalition district where commercial and residential development decisions directly affect different communities' economic opportunity and displacement risk. Development that generates tax revenue and new housing without benefiting existing residents — particularly renters and small-business owners in communities of color — will activate primary opposition from constituents who conclude the incumbent is managing development for outside investors rather than for existing community members. Equitable development frameworks, community benefits agreements, and anti-displacement enforcement are the concrete policy mechanisms that hold this coalition." },
          { name: "Coalition Maintenance in a 100% Democratic District", tag: "careful", why: "The unopposed 2024 victory — 100% Democratic, 0% Republican — is both an electoral asset and a governance risk: it reflects the strength of Democratic organization while potentially obscuring constituent dissatisfaction that would surface in a contested race. The district's demographic and economic diversity — 55% Black, 26% white, multiple immigrant communities, and split homeowner-renter population — means that intra-coalition satisfaction is the actual electoral variable. Any incumbent who mistakes the absence of Republican opposition for absence of political vulnerability will discover primary pressure from a challenger who mobilizes the coalition's neglected constituencies." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed Victory as Permission for Reduced Accountability", tag: "avoid", why: "A 100% Democratic result in a state with New York's political sophistication does not indicate that the incumbent has permanently secured the seat — it indicates that Republicans lack the infrastructure to mount a general-election challenge. The primary electorate, particularly in a high-turnout presidential year, can produce a credible progressive or community-focused challenger if the incumbent is perceived as taking the coalition for granted. Reduced constituent service, limited office hours, or governance priorities misaligned with the renter and working-class majority will activate primary opposition." },
          { name: "Allowing Demographic Minorities Within the District to Become Invisible", tag: "avoid", why: "The 55% Black population can create an illusion of demographic dominance that obscures the 26% white, 8.67% Hispanic, and 5.71% Asian communities whose economic and civic concerns are equally present and real. Development decisions that center Black community priorities while neglecting Hispanic small-business districts, or that ignore Asian immigrant community economic mobility concerns, create vulnerable flanks for a primary challenger. In a 100% Democratic district, the challenger who credibly engages the neglected communities can mobilize a winning coalition from within the primary." }
