          { name: "Asian & Hispanic Community Economic Integration", tag: "lean-into", why: "Combined, Asian and Hispanic/Latino residents comprise 32.63% of NY-AD-64 — a substantial demographic plurality that reflects suburban economic diversification and professional-class integration. At $95,113 median income, these communities are not economically marginalized; they are property-holding, tax-paying constituents whose concerns center on educational opportunity, professional mobility, and protection of property values. Authentic engagement with these communities on their own terms — not as affinity groups, but as significant portions of the constituency — is a prerequisite for any candidate seeking to expand beyond the current 100% Republican margin." },
          { name: "Renter Community & Affordability Pressure", tag: "careful", why: "At 37.6% renter rate in a $95,113 median income district, NY-AD-64 contains a significant cohort of younger adults and service-sector workers for whom housing costs are a direct squeeze on their economic security despite the district's overall affluence. This community is older than typical college-age rental cohorts (42.4 median age) and likely includes both young professionals and older adults on fixed incomes. Their concerns about housing affordability and rental market stability are real and present, even in an affluent district, and represent a potential source of electoral vulnerability if ignored." },
          { name: "Running Unopposed as a Strategic Ceiling", tag: "avoid", why: "Tannousis's 2024 unopposed victory is not evidence of an invulnerable seat — it is evidence of a district where the opposition has not yet invested sufficient resources to mount a challenge. A 100% result reflects the absence of competitive opposition, not the presence of unanimous support. In a district with 37.6% renters, 32.63% Asian and Hispanic residents, and meaningful college-educated professional sophistication, the actual range of political opinion is broader than the unopposed result suggests." },
          { name: "Treating the District as Homogeneous Affluent Consensus", tag: "avoid", why: "The $95,113 income and 62.48% white majority can obscure the meaningful stratification within NY-AD-64: renters versus homeowners, younger professionals versus established families, Asian and Hispanic residents with distinct economic and cultural priorities, and college-educated professionals whose fiscal conservatism may not align with social conservatism. Any candidate who campaigns this seat as though it is a unified affluent bloc will miss the constituency tensions that a sophisticated challenger could exploit to fragment the current coalition." }
