          { name: "Wage Growth & Economic Mobility for Working Families", tag: "lean-into", why: "With median income under $49k and 39.5% college attainment in a 37.9-year-old district, this is a working-class constituency evaluating whether wages, benefits, and job quality are improving or stagnating. Labor policy, minimum wage, benefits access, and workforce development are the concrete frames through which working families assess whether government is delivering. Generic economic messaging will not move persuadable voters; specific commitment to wage and employment quality does." },
          { name: "Engagement of Distinct Hispanic and Black Communities", tag: "careful", why: "While the 69.21% combined Hispanic and Black population makes this a majority-minority district, these two communities have distinct economic concerns, organizing capacity, and political relationships that can differ substantially. The Latino community and Black community in NY-AD-68 may prioritize different issues — immigration status and workplace access for Latino constituents, criminal justice and educational equity for Black constituents. A campaign that treats them as a single bloc rather than distinct constituencies misses opportunity to build deep relationships and understand what material delivery looks like to each." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed Status as Strategic Stability", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed 2024 result does not signal durable partisan advantage or constituent satisfaction; it reflects the current absence of a Republican challenger with resources or community credibility. In a district where 88% of voters are renters with limited asset accumulation and $48,685 income, an experienced challenger who campaigns on housing affordability failure and wage stagnation would be contesting material ground where many constituents are genuinely frustrated. Assuming the seat is safe because no one ran in 2024 is a strategic error." },
          { name: "Generic Messaging About Prosperity or Democratic Accomplishment", tag: "avoid", why: "A constituency where the median renter household earns under $49k and 39.5% lack college credentials will not respond to abstract messaging about economic growth or party affiliation. If housing costs are rising faster than wages and displacement pressure is real, constituents will perceive generic prosperity messaging as disconnected from their material reality. Credibility in this district is built on specific, concrete evidence of delivery on housing affordability, wage growth, and neighborhood stability — not ideological positioning." }
