          { name: "Hispanic Voter Coalition Maintenance", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 41.08% Hispanic plurality is the Democratic baseline in NY-AD-90, and the 56-44 margin depends on maintaining 65-70% Hispanic support rather than assuming it. Hispanic voters in this district include working-class households, small-business owners, and renters whose immediate concerns are economic mobility, housing costs, and whether public services function effectively. Hispanic support for the incumbent is conditional on economic delivery, not identity-based, and any perception that Democratic governance is failing on jobs, housing, or small-business support creates opening for Republican messaging that emphasizes competence and economic relief." },
          { name: "Public Service Delivery & Visible Governance Performance", tag: "careful", why: "At 39.8 median age and 45.7% renter rate, NY-AD-90 constituents evaluate government primarily on whether visible services (transit, schools, public safety, housing regulation) function effectively rather than on partisan principle. White voters at 36% of the district deliver 44% Republican support, indicating this constituency remains persuadable on competence grounds if the incumbent appears to be failing. Any visible failure in school performance, transit reliability, or public safety becomes a Republican attack surface that is more powerful than partisan identity arguments in a district where material service delivery is the actual voting concern." },
          { name: "Ideological Framing Over Economic Materialism", tag: "avoid", why: "Democratic messaging that leads with identity or ideological positioning—rather than concrete cost-of-living solutions—fails in NY-AD-90 where renters are focused on affordability, Hispanic voters are evaluating economic opportunity, and white voters are assessing service delivery. A campaign that emphasizes partisan or identity-based themes while failing to demonstrate concrete progress on housing costs, transit, schools, or small-business support invites Republican messaging that repositions the contest as a referendum on incumbent competence rather than partisan choice." },
          { name: "Assuming Hispanic Voter Loyalty Without Affirmative Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "Hispanic voters at 41% plurality require active constituent service, economic opportunity demonstration, and affirmative engagement on small-business and workforce development—not assumed loyalty. If incumbent engagement with Hispanic constituencies becomes perfunctory or if Republican messaging can credibly argue that Hispanic households are worse off economically, Hispanic support slips from 70% to 60%, which is mathematically sufficient to flip the seat. Treating Hispanic voters as a stable coalition rather than an active constituency requiring continuous economic and policy affirmation is the single highest strategic vulnerability for the Democratic incumbent in this district." }
