          { name: "Treating 53% as a Safe Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 53-47 result is not a majority—it is a plurality of contested voters in a district where 47% actively chose someone else and substantial swing constituencies exist within the 53%. Any incumbent or challenger who campaigns this seat as though 53% represents a durable hold is arithmetically wrong and strategically vulnerable. The combination of a mixed-income, multi-racial district with meaningful renter and housing-cost pressure means that both incumbent and challenger are one credible economic message away from a competitive outcome." },
          { name: "Ignoring Economic Concerns in Favor of Ideological Positioning", tag: "avoid", why: "NY-AD-100 is a working- and middle-class district with 27% college education—below national average—where voters evaluate on material economic delivery rather than abstract ideological positioning. An incumbent or challenger who campaigns primarily on cultural or identity issues rather than housing affordability, healthcare costs, wage growth, and small business support will miss the actual concerns of the 35.3% renter constituency and the white working-class voters who are currently split between the parties. This district votes on whether government is delivering for them; ideology is secondary to economics." }
