          { name: "Hispanic/Latino Economic Mobility and Workforce Development", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 27.37% of the district, Hispanic/Latino residents represent the second-largest demographic group and a constituency with direct economic stakes in job quality, wage growth, and skill-based employment pathways. With median household income at $79,280 and 43.3% renter rate—indicating a community with housing cost pressures despite moderate income—Hispanic families are actively evaluating whether government is delivering economic opportunity. This is a community whose turnout and candidate preference are contingent on perceiving concrete legislative attention to workforce development, small business access, and housing affordability, not inherited partisan loyalty." },
          { name: "Renter-Majority Housing Affordability and Tenant Protection", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 43.3% of the district renting, nearly half the constituency has direct economic exposure to housing costs and tenant-landlord relationships. At a median household income of $79,280, renter households are spending a substantial share of earnings on housing, making rent stabilization, eviction prevention, and affordable housing production material governance issues rather than abstract policy concerns. A representative who engages this renter-majority with specific legislative solutions around housing affordability commands the credibility this constituency's economic reality demands." },
          { name: "Black Community Civic Investment and Economic Inclusion", tag: "careful", why: "At 18.58% of the district, Black residents represent a meaningful minority constituency whose economic mobility, healthcare access, and civic representation require active legislative engagement. In a district where the dominant demographic story centers on Hispanic/Latino residents and the large renter population, the Black community's specific needs around workforce equity, business access, and educational opportunity can become invisible without deliberate constituent attention. This is a community whose participation and support depend on perceiving that the representative is serving them distinctly, not absorbing their concerns into a broader minority narrative." },
          { name: "Treating 100% Unopposed Victory as a Mandate or Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "The 2024 unopposed result reflects a specific political moment—likely Republican candidate recruitment failure, weak party infrastructure, or demographic shifts that have fundamentally altered the district's competitive terrain—not necessarily durable constituent satisfaction. An unopposed victory is an opportunity to build authentic constituent relationships and a legislative record of concrete delivery, not permission to govern based on inherited partisan alignment or assume that absence of Republican opposition indicates stable support across the full constituency." },
          { name: "Ignoring or Minimizing the Asian Population's Civic and Economic Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "Though only 2.15% of the district, the Asian population is likely concentrated in specific neighborhoods with distinct economic profiles, small business activity, and immigration-related policy concerns that deserve specific representative attention. Even small minority communities in New York City often represent significant economic stakeholders and civic constituencies whose concerns around business licensing, immigration access, and professional credentialing can have outsized policy impact if neglected or incorporated only generically into broader constituent service." }
