          { name: "Street-Level Quality-of-Life Governance", tag: "lean-into", why: "A district of property-owning professionals at $104,978 median income with 73% white and 47.4% college-educated plurality evaluates representatives primarily on concrete local delivery: street cleanliness, pedestrian safety, noise regulation, and responsive constituent service. The relatively low 27.4% renter rate means the majority has direct property-value stakes in neighborhood conditions. This is not abstract positioning — it is the material governance that holds or loses 58-42 margins in upper-income Manhattan districts." },
          { name: "Responsive Constituent Service & Accountability", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $104,978 income with 47.4% college education, this is a constituency accustomed to institutional responsiveness and clear communication about how elected officials deploy resources and deliver results. The 42% opposition in a Democratic-registration district is likely concentrated among voters who believe local governance is unresponsive to their specific concerns — long wait times for pothole repair, unaddressed noise complaints, poor school performance. Building and defending 58-42 requires active, visible constituent engagement, not the assumption that Democratic registration alone will hold the vote." },
          { name: "Minority Community Economic Advancement & Equity", tag: "careful", why: "The 9.88% Asian, 6.5% Black, and 4.99% Hispanic populations represent meaningful minority communities whose economic mobility, healthcare access, and equitable access to neighborhood investment are distinct from the concerns of affluent property-owning professionals. These communities can become invisible in a district-wide narrative focused on street conditions and school quality. A representative who engages minority communities with specific policies on business development, healthcare access, and economic opportunity — rather than token acknowledgment — strengthens the Democratic margin by making these constituencies feel genuinely represented." },
          { name: "Anti-Development, Anti-Growth Housing Obstructionism", tag: "avoid", why: "While the property-owning majority values neighborhood stability, adopting an absolutist anti-development position alienates younger renters, minority communities seeking homeownership opportunity, and the broader city's need for housing supply. The 27.4% renter rate, while below average, still represents substantial number of constituency members priced out of ownership; messaging that pits property owners against housing advocates will fragment the Democratic base and activate opposition." },
          { name: "Treating Affluent White Homeowners as the Only Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "A representative who campaigns and governs as though the 73% white, affluent, college-educated property-owning plurality is the entire district will lose credibility with the 26.37% minority population and activate organized opposition from communities that perceive themselves as peripheral to local governance. The 58% margin was built by serving multiple constituencies simultaneously; defaulting to homeowner-only messaging is a path to erosion." }
