          { name: "Suburban Service Delivery & Local Quality of Life", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 28.4% college-educated adults and a median household income in the mid-$70,000s, NY-AD-114 is a suburban working- and middle-class community where schools, local infrastructure, public safety, and water/sewer systems are the direct service-delivery concerns that drive constituent satisfaction. The low renter rate (21.8%) and high homeownership signal constituents with long-term community stakes who evaluate their representative on whether local institutions are being maintained and improved. Legislative focus on education funding, infrastructure investment, and municipal fiscal health directly addresses what this electorate actually cares about." },
          { name: "The Absence of Partisan Competition", tag: "careful", why: "Simpson won unopposed in 2024 with the Democratic candidate polling at 0% — a result that reflects either overwhelming constituent satisfaction with current representation or a complete collapse of Democratic party infrastructure and candidate recruitment in this district. The distinction is strategically crucial: an unopposed win driven by genuine constituent loyalty is durable, while one driven by opposition party dysfunction is vulnerable to a credible challenger from either direction if circumstances change. Any incumbent should validate whether the 100% result reflects true consolidation of the district or simply the absence of an organized alternative." },
          { name: "Dismissing the 91% White Constituency as Monolithic", tag: "avoid", why: "At 91.18% white, NY-AD-114 is demographically homogeneous, but homogeneous demographics do not produce homogeneous interests or voting behavior — particularly in a suburban New York context where education levels, income stability, and tenure in the community vary significantly. Assuming that 91% white voters automatically respond to appeals based on identity politics rather than material household concerns is a strategic error that misses the actual composition of the district: older homeowners, working parents concerned about schools, households managing suburban living costs. Any candidate who frames the district's whiteness as the central political fact rather than addressing its actual governance concerns will underestimate both the sophistication of the electorate and the potential for internal fracture." },
          { name: "Taking Unopposed Status as Permanent Condition", tag: "avoid", why: "A 100% unopposed victory is a snapshot, not a structural guarantee — and in New York state assembly races, demographic and political circumstances can shift rapidly. The incumbent should treat the unopposed status as a temporary advantage that requires active constituent engagement to maintain, not as evidence of a permanently safe seat. New suburban districts with aging homeowner bases and rising cost-of-living pressures have historically proven vulnerable to challenger campaigns focused on local economic security when incumbent representatives become disconnected from constituent needs or fail to deliver on basic service-delivery expectations." }
