          { name: "Property Tax & School Funding", tag: "lean-into", why: "NY-AD-122 is a district of affluent homeowners (75% non-renter, $75,614 median income, 42.9 median age) for whom property tax burden and school district adequacy are the primary material concerns affecting household economics and quality of life. Rising property taxes and perceptions of school underfunding are the most reliable drivers of swing voting in demographically stable suburban constituencies. A legislative agenda that addresses tax fairness, school funding sustainability, and the fiscal relationship between homeowners and local government directly engages the decision-making criteria of this district's dominant constituency." },
          { name: "Suburban Infrastructure & Local Governance", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 75% homeownership and a median age of 42.9, NY-AD-122 is a mature suburban community where road maintenance, water system reliability, emergency services response times, and municipal budget transparency are the concrete governance issues that shape voter satisfaction. The 37% Democratic opposition in a district with minimal demographic diversity suggests that some portion of the Anglo homeowner majority is evaluating the incumbent on actual service delivery rather than partisan identity alone. Demonstrating competence on unglamorous but materially important municipal governance — and showing fiscal discipline in budget management — is how a representative holds this seat against persuadable swing voters." },
          { name: "Engagement With Demographic Minorities", tag: "careful", why: "While Hispanic (3.52%), Black (1.58%), and Asian (1.38%) populations total only 6.48%, they are present and deserve constituent service on their specific needs — economic opportunity, small business access, healthcare equity, and cultural recognition — rather than being treated as invisible. Even in small numbers, these communities can be organized if they perceive neglect; a representative who demonstrates genuine engagement builds political insurance against opposition organizing and reflects authentic constituent service that strengthens the seat's durability." },
          { name: "Treating 63% as a Safe Majority Without Understanding Opposition", tag: "avoid", why: "A 63% result in a district with 90% demographic homogeneity and 75% homeownership should mathematically produce a 70%+ Republican margin; the presence of 37% opposition in a constituency with minimal swing demographics signals that some portion of the Anglo homeowner base is either persuadable or mobilized by specific incumbent performance issues. Any candidate who campaigns this seat as though 63% is a secure floor — without understanding what generated the 37% or whether it reflects addressable local governance failures — is managing a seat more fragile than the headline margin suggests." },
          { name: "Neglecting Fiscal Credibility Among Affluent Homeowners", tag: "avoid", why: "A district with $75,614 median income and 75% homeownership evaluates representatives on fiscal discipline and smart spending discipline, not on partisan rhetoric or cultural positioning. An incumbent or challenger who offers abstract conservative messaging without concrete answers to local tax burden, school funding sustainability, and municipal budget management will lose persuadable swing voters in the homeowner majority to opposition candidates who offer specific fiscal solutions. This constituency has the income and education to demand sophisticated fiscal governance, not bumper-sticker economics." }
