          { name: "Treating 59% as a Stable or Safe Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 59-41 result is a solid win but not a durable landslide in a district where the median income is $56,507, 44% are renters, and 66% lack college education. These constituencies are not inherited Democratic voters—they are working-class households evaluating on material economic outcomes. An incumbent who campaigns as though 59% represents a comfortable hold rather than a coalition that must be actively consolidated will find the district unexpectedly competitive if a credible challenger articulates a coherent message on housing, jobs, or local service failure." },
          { name: "Abstract or Ideological Messaging Over Material Economic Outcomes", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district with 44% renters, $56,507 income, and 36.8-year median age, voters are evaluating on whether their economic situation is improving, not on abstract policy positions or ideological purity. An incumbent who leads with cultural or ideological messaging rather than demonstrating concrete results on housing affordability, wage growth, or local job creation will lose the district's non-college-educated and renter-majority constituencies to a challenger who frames the race as a referendum on kitchen-table economics. This is a district that rewards material governance and punishes perceived disconnection from working-class economic anxiety." }
