          "Consolidate non-college-educated voters (65.8% of the district) through specific, concrete messaging on wages, benefits, job quality, and local economic opportunity—avoid abstract policy language and focus on whether government is delivering material relief for working households.",
          "Engage renters and younger adults (median age 36.8) on the intersection of housing costs and wage stagnation; build continuous constituent contact infrastructure in neighborhoods with high renter concentration to prevent invisible disaffection from emerging as a surprise in the next cycle.",
          "Avoid any messaging that suggests the incumbent is indifferent to or dismissive of working-class economic anxiety—in a district this economically constrained, perceived disconnection from material household concerns is the single most reliable path for a challenger to break through."
