          { name: "Healthcare Access & Cost in a Working-Class District", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $66,818 median income and 35.4% college attainment, NY-AD-128 is a working-class constituency for whom healthcare cost and access are material economic security concerns. Many households in this range have either unstable insurance or high-deductible coverage; healthcare cost shocks are capable of triggering financial instability. Legislation addressing prescription drug costs, insurance affordability, and access to preventive care in underserved neighborhoods directly reflects what voters in this district need from their representative." },
          { name: "Multiethnic Community Engagement & Specific Constituent Attention", tag: "careful", why: "With 62.12% white, 20.63% Black, 7.55% Hispanic, and 3.91% Asian residents, plus a 6.63% unaccounted demographic gap, NY-AD-128 is genuinely multiethnic with no single community constituting an overwhelming majority. Each community has distinct concerns around employment, healthcare access, immigration policy, and community safety; treating the district as a unified constituency risks rendering specific concerns invisible. Authentic engagement with each community through regular constituent attention, community organization partnerships, and responsive governance is both an electoral and a governance obligation." },
          { name: "Treating 63% as a Durable, Safe Majority Rather Than an Active Working Margin", tag: "avoid", why: "A 63-37 result in a district with 37.7% renters, $66,818 median income, and material economic exposure is not a safe seat — it is a working margin held by alignment between the incumbent and a constituency that evaluates on economic delivery. If voters perceive that the representative is not delivering on housing, healthcare, or employment concerns, that 63% can contract rapidly; a challenger with a credible message on material economic concerns can mobilize opposition in this district." },
          { name: "Overlooking the 6.63% Demographic Gap & Associated Population Segments", tag: "avoid", why: "The gap between reported Census categories and 100% represents approximately 1 in 15 residents — likely including recent immigrants, undocumented residents, and other population segments less visible to Census enumeration. In a district where housing, healthcare, and economic mobility are universal concerns, ignoring or failing to actively engage this population segment means ceding constituent relationship-building to challengers and community organizations that do prioritize these residents. This is both a governance failure and an electoral vulnerability." }
