          { name: "Treating Suburban Affluence as Politically Settled", tag: "avoid", why: "Affluent suburban districts with strong property values and excellent schools can appear electorally stable, but they are sensitive to shifts in tax policy, school governance, and whether government investment feels responsive to constituent priorities. A representative who campaigns this seat as though 65% represents a stable coalition and neglects to actively defend school funding priorities, tax fairness, or the material concerns of the 21.8% renter population risks allowing the Democratic opposition to consolidate an alternative narrative around economic mobility and generational fairness. The margin is comfortable but not fireproof against a challenger who speaks specifically to younger renters or education funding with more conviction." }
