          "The district's income level ($81,086 median) places it solidly upper-middle-class, and the 26.5% renter rate indicates a mix of established homeowners and younger or more transient professionals — a composition that fragments along education, life-stage, and values lines rather than consolidating neatly into a single partisan coalition. The combination of college education (32.5%), moderate-to-high income, suburban location, and age (41.6 median, older than metro average but not elderly) creates the exact demographic conditions under which suburban voters in New York have shown willingness to split tickets or shift parties in response to specific issues: education funding, property tax management, reproductive rights, and governance competence. An incumbent who treats this as a safe hold based on the 2024 result will be vulnerable if a credible Democratic challenger emerges with a message that resonates with college-educated professionals and younger professionals concerned about reproductive freedom, education quality, or local tax burden."
