          "The unopposed victory, while electorally comfortable, creates a strategic vulnerability: the incumbent has not faced organized opposition in a cycle, meaning neither candidate nor party infrastructure has been stress-tested against a credible challenger. An opposition candidate who identifies and speaks directly to the economic concerns of the renter plurality (housing affordability, tenant rights, eviction protection), the college-educated plurality (student debt, underemployment, professional opportunity), and the working-class majority (job security, healthcare access, property tax burden) could rapidly organize a competitive challenge. The incumbent's 2024 performance tells us who showed up for the incumbent, not who might be persuadable or mobilizable by a well-resourced alternative. In a district where 65% of the population lacks a four-year degree and one-third are renters, messaging that centers on housing security, wage growth, and healthcare access — rather than abstract progressive positioning — is the baseline for genuine constituent engagement. Any candidate, incumbent or challenger, who treats this district as safely Democratic rather than as a constituency requiring active economic argument will be vulnerable to an opponent who takes the time to understand why renters and working-class homeowners in this district might rationally prefer a different representative."
