          "The strategic vulnerability for the Democratic incumbent is concentrated in the homeowner and higher-income segments of the 67% non-renter majority, while the swing opportunity lies in mobilizing the 33.4% renter constituency — disproportionately younger, less wealthy, and more exposed to housing costs and service inflation — around housing affordability and economic security. The 31.6% college-educated population is large enough to matter but not dominant; this is not a college-town district where credentialed professionals automatically align Democratic. Instead, this is a middle-income suburban community where homeowners are managing mortgage or property tax burdens at $67,753 income, renters are watching housing costs consume an increasing share of income, and both are asking whether government is delivering value or adding cost. Any Republican challenger who can articulate a concrete housing affordability agenda — supply-side zoning reform, tax relief for renters or young homebuyers, streamlined local permitting — has a direct opening to the 49% who voted Republican in 2024 and the marginal homeowners who are cost-conscious enough to consider switching. Any Democratic incumbent who assumes the 51% is a durable coalition without sustained engagement of the renter constituency and cost-conscious homeowners will find the margin slip further in a higher-turnout or more polarized election cycle."
