          "However, the apparent solidity of the 62% result masks a specific vulnerability: this is an aging district ($87,676 income notwithstanding) facing the rising fixed costs of healthcare, long-term care, and property maintenance on fixed or slowly-growing incomes. The 44.6 median age, combined with high homeownership and upper-middle-class income, creates a constituency whose primary economic anxiety is not about absolute poverty but about whether their accumulated assets and stable incomes will survive the cost pressures of aging in place. Healthcare costs, property tax burden, municipal service quality, and the viability of senior services in an aging community are the material governance issues that activate this constituency — not abstract ideology. The 38% opposition is unlikely to be persuaded toward the incumbent through partisan messaging; it is more likely to grow if the incumbent is perceived as inattentive to the specific economic security concerns of older, asset-holding homeowners. Any challenger capable of articulating a concrete agenda on healthcare affordability, property tax fairness, and aging-in-place services could credibly threaten the margin, particularly if demographic change gradually shifts the district's composition toward younger renters or if economic conditions strain fixed-income households."
