          { name: "Public Education Quality & K-12 to Post-Secondary Pipeline", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 37% college attainment rate indicates a constituency engaged with education as a pathway to economic mobility, and the 40.2-year median age suggests many constituents have school-age children or grandchildren in the K-12 system. School funding adequacy, teacher compensation, curriculum quality, and the K-12 to college/career transition are the material education concerns for a middle-class and professional constituency that stakes economic futures on education outcomes. Legislative attention to these specific issues—not generic education rhetoric—builds durable constituent relationships with families whose economic security directly depends on school quality." },
          { name: "Criminal Justice System Responsiveness & Racial Equity in Policing & Prosecution", tag: "careful", why: "A 45.99% Black constituency lives with over-policing, racial disparities in prosecution and sentencing, and systemic inequities in the criminal justice system as lived reality rather than abstract policy concern. This constituency is politically engaged on these issues and evaluates representatives on concrete legislative action—bail reform, discovery rules, conviction integrity, and police accountability. An incumbent cannot take this issue for granted; credible challengers will mobilize Black voters by highlighting specific instances of unequal justice in the district and offering concrete legislative remedies. Demonstrating measurable progress on criminal justice equity is essential to maintaining Black voter mobilization and trust." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed Status as Electoral Security", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed 2024 victory reflects current party strength and opposition weakness, not permanent electoral invulnerability. Future cycles may produce credible Republican, Working Families Party, or independent challengers capable of mobilizing disaffected constituents if the incumbent has neglected service delivery or become perceived as unresponsive. An incumbent who campaigns as if the 100% margin is a durable floor rather than a current snapshot is strategically misreading the district's political reality and will be vulnerable to well-organized challenge from either conventional or third-party opposition." },
          { name: "Confusing Democratic Party Registration with Constituent Loyalty", tag: "avoid", why: "Democratic affiliation in NY-SD-14 is strong and durable, but party registration does not equal active engagement or enthusiasm for any particular incumbent or legislative agenda. Constituents in a 45.99% Black, college-educated, $94,855-income district make votes based on demonstrated responsiveness to material concerns—housing, education, healthcare, criminal justice—not partisan default. An incumbent who relies on Democratic identity to sustain electoral performance without delivering measurable progress on the issues constituents actually care about will find that registration advantage insufficient to overcome organized challenger opposition or primary challenge from within-party alternatives." }
