          { name: "Taking 77% for Granted or Treating It as a Floor Rather Than a Coalition That Requires Active Management", tag: "avoid", why: "The 77% margin is substantial, but it rests on the active coalition across Hispanic, Asian, and White educated voters—each with distinct priorities that cannot be bundled into generic progressive positioning. A representative who campaigns as though the 77% is a stable partisan base and who neglects specific engagement with Hispanic economic mobility concerns, Asian education and property-value priorities, or renter housing needs risks the coalition's disaggregation. In a district this demographically diverse, the margin is only as durable as the incumbents's capacity to serve each community's specific concerns." },
          { name: "Ignoring the White College-Educated Coalition or Treating It as an Automatic Base", tag: "avoid", why: "The 27.47% White population, with 33.1% college attainment and $88,462 median income, is a significant portion of the coalition and evaluates on governance competence, education quality, property values, and neighborhood investment—not on abstract progressive ideology. A representative who writes off this community or assumes it will support any Democratic candidate risks a credibility gap and potential defection, particularly to a challenger who emphasizes competence and results over partisan positioning. This community's support is part of the 77%, not an entitlement to it." }
