          "The Asian plurality at 44.08%—more than 12 points above the white population and nearly 2.5 times the Hispanic share—signals a district shaped by specific immigrant and second-generation Asian communities whose voting behavior is notoriously data-resistant to partisan stereotype. Asian voters in high-cost urban districts with high renter rates respond to concrete governance on housing affordability, school quality, public safety, and economic mobility rather than to broad ideological messaging. The 55% Republican result reflects Chan's demonstrated ability to communicate with this constituency on issues it cares about; a Democratic challenger who assumes Asian voters are a bloc to be claimed through identity messaging rather than through specific policy engagement on housing, education, and economic opportunity will misread both the district and the demographic group. At $66,649 median income with 66.8% renters and only 29.9% college education, this is a constituency economically pressured by the cost of urban living, and any candidate who does not lead with concrete answers to housing affordability and tenant protection will cede ground to an opponent who does."
