          { name: "Education as Economic Mobility & Labor Market Access", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 37.4% college attainment rate signals significant post-secondary education engagement, but $76,962 median income indicates that credential does not universally translate to professional-class economic outcomes, particularly for the Hispanic plurality (42.59%) for whom education represents intergenerational mobility opportunity. Student debt burden, wage stagnation relative to education level, and the gap between credential and job quality are the material concerns driving electoral behavior in a young, educated, economically constrained constituency. A legislative agenda that treats education as an ongoing investment requiring support—not a completed pathway—reflects the actual economic experience of most constituents." },
          { name: "Hispanic Plurality Consolidation & Latino Political Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "The 42.59% Hispanic population is a plurality, not a majority, and their electoral alignment with the Democratic incumbent is driven by immigration policy, labor market positioning, and social policy—not by deep satisfaction with economic outcomes in a housing-stressed, renter-majority constituency. While Hispanic voters are reliable Democratic voters at the state and federal level, a state Senate race in which material economic conditions (housing, wages) are not improving can create space for a challenger to make inroads by offering concrete economic relief. Consolidating Hispanic voter loyalty requires moving beyond cultural or identity positioning to demonstrating specific legislative results on housing, wages, and economic mobility." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed Victory as Evidence of Partisan Strength", tag: "avoid", why: "Salazar won unopposed in 2024, but this reflects the absence of a competitive challenger, not overwhelming constituent satisfaction or insurmountable Democratic strength. In a district where 40-45% of voters would normally appear in opposition, unopposed victory is a strategic vulnerability masquerading as strength—any credible challenger addressing housing affordability, education ROI, or wage stagnation will find a persuadable electorate. The incumbent's strategic error would be to treat the unopposed result as permission to coast rather than as a signal to consolidate support on material economic concerns before opposition emerges." },
          { name: "Defaulting to Culture-War Positioning or Abstract Progressive Ideology", tag: "avoid", why: "NY-SD-18's Democratic alignment is driven by material economic concerns (housing, wages, education access) and immigration policy relevant to the Hispanic plurality—not by enthusiasm for culture-war positioning or abstract progressive ideology detached from household budget constraints. A young, educated, renter-majority constituency experiencing housing stress and wage stagnation will punish a legislator who addresses culture-war issues while ignoring rent, wages, or education costs. The strategic focus must remain relentlessly on material economic conditions, not on positioning that feels culturally coherent to progressive activists but disconnects from what constituents are worried about when rent is due." }
