          "Understand that unopposed status masks primary vulnerability: the 100-0 result reflects partisan alignment, not necessarily durable personal power; community organizations around housing, racial economic justice, and tenant rights are the actual loci of political energy in this district, and an incumbent who treats general election dominance as permission to ignore community-based organizing will face a motivated primary challenge.",
          "Address the working-class economic squeeze specifically: at $58,942 median income with 68.6% renting in Brooklyn, most households face compounding cost pressures on housing, transit, childcare, and healthcare; state-level policy on wage standards, childcare subsidy, transit affordability, and Medicaid access are the material governance levers that affect whether working families can actually afford to stay in the district.",
          "Engage the 21.2% college-attainment constituency on educational pathways and workforce development: limited college access is both an economic mobility barrier and a demographic signal of a constituency that depends on vocational training, community college access, and union apprenticeships; state investment in these pathways is a direct economic justice issue for a district where four in five adults do not hold a four-year degree."
