          { name: "Education Investment & Economic Mobility", tag: "lean-into", why: "With only 29.4% college attainment and a working-age constituency concentrated in low-wage sectors, public schools and early childhood investment are the primary institutional pathways to economic mobility for the district's children. School funding adequacy, teacher compensation and support, college access programs, and vocational pathways are constituent expectations in a district where educational credential attainment directly determines economic future. Visible, sustained investment in school infrastructure and support is both a governance obligation and a political necessity in a majority-Latino, substantial-Black district where public education is the fundamental institution connecting present deprivation to future opportunity." },
          { name: "Wage Standards & Labor Market Power", tag: "careful", why: "The $43,071 median household income reflects service, healthcare, and manual labor employment where wage stagnation and precarious scheduling are structural realities for working families. Paid family leave, minimum wage standards, predictable scheduling, and workplace safety protections are material policy concerns for a constituency whose income is insufficient to build assets despite full-time work. These issues have potential to mobilize constituent engagement, but only if tied to specific legislation and enforcement rather than abstract labor advocacy — the constituency cares about whether their hours are predictable and their wages sufficient, not about labor solidarity rhetoric disconnected from their own material conditions." },
          { name: "Treating the 82% Supermajority as Electorally Safe", tag: "avoid", why: "The supermajority reflects structural Democratic alignment in a heavily minority district, not constituent satisfaction with service delivery or representation. An 82% result can create the political perception of an invulnerable seat, leading to neglect of constituent engagement, reduced responsiveness to housing and school demands, and assumption that the base will remain loyal regardless of performance. In a low-income renter-majority district where material conditions are directly observable — housing affordability, school quality, wage adequacy — an incumbent who becomes identified with service delivery failure or constituent neglect can find that supermajority eroded rapidly by a challenger with genuine urgency around unmet need." },
          { name: "Treating Latino and Black Communities as a Unified Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "While the district is 52% Latino and 32% Black, these communities have distinct economic circumstances, historical relationships to institutions, and policy priorities that require separate constituent attention. Economic mobility for Latino working families and wealth-building disparities in the Black community are related but not identical issues; healthcare access, education equity, and criminal justice concerns carry different weight across these communities. A representative who treats the Latino-plus-Black sum as a unified constituency rather than engaging each community's specific needs and leadership independently will miss opportunities for meaningful constituent relationship and create perceived neglect that a competitor can exploit." }
