          { name: "Defaulting to Partisan Messaging Over Constituent Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "In high-income suburban districts with significant college education (36%), voters evaluate candidates primarily on policy sophistication, fiscal management, and evidence-based reasoning rather than partisan identity or abstract ideology. Campaigns built on partisan consolidation rather than specific constituent remedies—addressing homeowner tax burden, renter housing affordability, school funding adequacy, or healthcare access—will lose persuadable swing voters who are explicitly looking for competent governance rather than partisan loyalty. The 45% opposition reflects a constituency that has either lost faith in the incumbent's competence or concluded the opposition offers a superior policy direction; partisan messaging does not reverse that loss." }
