          "The district's economic profile—$69,414 median household income, 27.9% college attainment, 29.1% renter rate—describes a stable, homeowner-dominant rural constituency neither affluent nor economically stressed. This is precisely the profile most vulnerable to erosion by challenger recruitment, local governance failures, or shifts in how constituent needs are framed. The 42.8 median age and 27.9% college attainment suggest a community where younger adults with post-secondary education may face limited local economic opportunity, creating a potential opening for messaging around workforce development, housing affordability, or regional economic revitalization. Without recent competitive pressure, there is no public evidence of how these constituencies would respond to a well-resourced challenger who invests in ground organization, local media presence, or issue-focused campaigning on economic mobility, agricultural policy, or rural infrastructure investment."
