          { name: "Non-College-Educated Workforce Economic Mobility", tag: "lean-into", why: "With only 27.5% college attainment, 72.5% of constituents lack a four-year degree and are the primary constituency vulnerable to wage stagnation and benefits erosion in New York's economy. Technical education, apprenticeship programs, direct workforce development investment, and advocacy for wage competitiveness are the policy areas where an incumbent can demonstrate concrete commitment to working families who do not have college credentials. This population is electorally decisive in suburban districts and responds powerfully to messaging about whether state government is materially improving their circumstances." },
          { name: "Understanding the True Competitive Landscape", tag: "careful", why: "An unopposed 2024 victory should not generate confidence about 2026; the demographic composition of NY-SD-62 — moderate income, non-college-educated majority, significant renter population — is precisely the suburban middle-class base that has become politically volatile in New York's recent electoral cycles. The lack of a defensive record against economic-anxiety messaging means opposition will have room to define the incumbent's record on cost-of-living issues before the incumbent has demonstrated strength in those areas. A credible Democratic challenger will arrive in 2026 with a developed case about economic neglect; the incumbent must anticipate this and build a proactive record before opposition messaging takes hold." },
          { name: "Assuming the Unopposed Victory Reflects Durable Electoral Strength", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed election in 2024 reflects temporary Democratic weakness or resource allocation, not fundamental voter alignment in a district where median income is $70,242, renters comprise 27.4%, and non-college-educated workers are 72.5% of the population. These demographics indicate vulnerability to economic-anxiety messaging, and the lack of a 2024 test means the incumbent has no proven defense against opposition on housing, healthcare, or wage issues. Entering 2026 with the confidence of an unopposed victory rather than the humility of recognizing latent competitive weakness is a strategic error." },
          { name: "Treating College Attainment (27.5%) as a Signal of High Education Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "While 27.5% college attainment is above some rural baselines, it is meaningfully below the New York state average and signals a constituency where nearly three-quarters lack four-year degrees. Positioning education policy around college access or university funding will miss the 72.5% of constituents for whom high school, technical training, and workforce pathways are the relevant education issues. A representative who defaults to college-focused education messaging is implicitly deprioritizing the educational and economic concerns of the district's actual majority." }
