          "Engage the non-college-educated majority (72.5%) with material policy specificity: workforce development, technical education funding, and direct support for workers without four-year degrees are the policy areas where an incumbent can build authentic constituent relationships and insulate against the 'out of touch with working families' framing that opposition will deploy.",
          "Address renter-specific governance: tenant protections, rental assistance, and affordable housing development are not fringe Democratic issues — they are direct constituent needs for nearly 28% of the district and a leading issue space where opposition will make the case that current representation is ignoring their economic vulnerability.",
          "Recognize that unopposed victory masked competitive weakness: without a 2026 opponent named or a defensive electoral record, assume the seat will attract a credible Democratic challenger and begin 2025 with messaging and constituent engagement as if facing opposition — not in confident maintenance mode."
