          "Address healthcare access and affordability as a material economic issue: for a working-age, wage-earning constituency at $51,617 income without college credentialing, healthcare costs and insurance access compete directly with rent and food for constrained household budgets — specific commitments on Medicaid availability, prescription drug costs, and preventive care access are more valuable than abstract healthcare rhetoric.",
          "Build authentic engagement with the Black community (29.2%), white working-class base, and Latino (9.7%) and Asian (6.7%) communities as distinct constituencies with specific concerns: avoid treating all non-white groups as a single bloc, and do not neglect white working-class constituents as though their vote is automatic — credibility with each community requires specific constituent attention and demonstrated responsiveness.",
          "Do not treat 66% as a durable majority: the 34% opposition in a working-class renter-majority district signals material dissatisfaction that can grow if the incumbent fails to deliver on cost-of-living commitments or if a credible challenger emerges — active constituent engagement, visible effectiveness on housing and healthcare, and regular community presence are prerequisites for holding the seat at or above 65% in a competitive statewide environment."
